<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panama</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Panama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Panama, officially the Republic of Panama, is a country on the narrow isthmus at the southern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>. Its Indigenous peoples were followed by Spanish colonists in the 16th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a> was founded in 1519. Panama broke away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1821 and joined Gran Colombia, then remained part of Colombia after that union dissolved in 1831. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> backing it seceded from Colombia in 1903, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> was built by the US Army Corps of Engineers between 1904 and 1914. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrijos-carter-treaties">Torrijos–Carter Treaties</a> of 1977 provided for transfer of the canal to Panama on 31 December 1999.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Panama</b> declared its independence from Spain in November 1821 and joined the republic of its own accord.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Scotland’s acceptance of the union was eased by the ‘Equivalent’, a payment of about £398,000 that partly compensated investors in the failed Darien colony in <b>Panama</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The National Federation of Coffee Growers, founded in 1927, organised marketing, and coffee earnings, together with the 25 million dollars the United States paid in compensation for <b>Panama</b> in the 1920s, financed railways, roads and the first factories, notably the textile mills of Medellín.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Republic of Colombia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1902 opponents of a Nicaraguan canal sent US senators Nicaraguan postage stamps showing Momotombo volcano in eruption; shortly afterwards the Senate chose <b>Panama</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1903 treaty granting the United States the Canal Zone was signed for <b>Panama</b> by a Frenchman, Philippe Bunau-Varilla, before the Panamanian delegation had reached Washington.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Silver travelled by the Armada del Mar del Sur from Callao to <b>Panama</b> and on to Seville, and by way of Acapulco and the Manila galleons to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
